WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.686
Different runtimes use different names, but several concepts appear again and again.

2
00:00:05.686 --> 00:00:10.515
Batch size describes how much work the runtime tries to process together.

3
00:00:10.515 --> 00:00:17.057
Microbatch size breaks larger work into smaller chunks so the runtime can fit within memory limits.

4
00:00:17.057 --> 00:00:24.924
Maximum batch tokens may limit the total number of prompt or generation tokens being processed across active requests.

5
00:00:24.924 --> 00:00:31.466
Maximum concurrent sequences controls how many active conversations or requests can be in flight.

6
00:00:31.466 --> 00:00:36.451
Context length controls how much prompt and history each request can carry.

7
00:00:36.451 --> 00:00:41.047
Thread count affects CPU execution and support work around the model.

8
00:00:41.047 --> 00:00:47.278
GPU layers or GPU offload settings decide how much of the model runs on the GPU instead of the CPU.

9
00:00:47.278 --> 00:00:52.340
Parallelism settings may affect how work is split across devices or streams.

10
00:00:52.340 --> 00:00:54.833
The confusing part is that names vary.

11
00:00:54.833 --> 00:00:57.792
One runtime may expose n_batch and n_ubatch.

12
00:00:57.792 --> 00:01:04.569
Another may talk about max batch tokens, continuous batching, slots, sequences, or scheduler limits.

13
00:01:04.569 --> 00:01:09.398
Do not assume two settings are identical just because they sound similar.

14
00:01:09.398 --> 00:01:14.616
Documentation, release notes, and measurement are part of the tuning process.

15
00:01:14.616 --> 00:01:25.754
A useful mental model is to ask what each setting controls: memory footprint, concurrency, prompt processing, decode scheduling, CPU support work, or GPU utilization.

16
00:01:25.754 --> 00:01:34.166
Once you know what kind of constraint a setting touches, you can make smaller changes and interpret the result more confidently.

17
00:01:34.166 --> 00:01:39.073
The setting name matters less than understanding the behavior it changes.

18
00:01:39.073 --> 00:01:44.058
When a runtime exposes many knobs, resist the urge to turn all of them at once.

19
00:01:44.058 --> 00:01:52.314
Start with the settings that define the workload boundary: context length, concurrency, and maximum prompt or batch tokens.

20
00:01:52.314 --> 00:01:56.909
Then tune the settings that affect how the work is divided internally.

21
00:01:56.909 --> 00:02:01.972
That order makes failures easier to interpret and rollback easier to manage.
